ProjectALife.DialogueShards = ProjectALife.DialogueShards or {}
ProjectALife.DialogueShards["radio_19"] = {
{"r9_so_d306_second_spring_list","sign_on",306,355,5,8,1,{"Marty","Morning, it's {time}, it's fifty-four degrees, and the dock door's been stood open since five because I could","Marty","Second spring, I didn't think I'd get to say a thing like second spring out loud, and now I've said it and it doesn't sound like much","Marty","Here's the morning list, two people to check on, both on the Ekron road - one trade at noon, and a man who wants to know if anybody's got a spare bicycle tube, thirty-two hundred","Lige","Three people to check on, I put one more in the column at four this morning","Marty","Three to check on - count your people, ninety-nine point six"}},
{"r5_trade_luanne_strawberries","trade",308,338,8,16,2,{"Caller - Luanne","Dee, the strawberries came in, the whole patch - two acres, I can't pick them all, and they're going to rot","Dee","Luanne, what's the trade?","Caller - Luanne","Pick two quarts, keep one, that's it, that's the deal - bring your own bucket","Marty","Folks, I haven't eaten a strawberry since last June","Caller - Luanne","Then you come too, Marty, the Tabor farm on Old Ekron Road, first gate past the white church - seven in the morning, before it gets hot","Dee","Bring your kids, wash the berries before they eat them - and pick with somebody watching the tree line","Caller - Luanne","My husband's up on the tractor with the rifle - he's watching - he'll wave","Marty","The Tabor farm, Old Ekron Road, seven AM - pick two, keep one, I'll be the one eating more than I pick"}},
{"r3_host_d310_straight_as_a_ruler","host",310,400,0,23,1,{"Dee","Kayla sutured her first real laceration today, a man from Riverside caught his forearm on a sheet of roofing tin - nine stitches, straight as a ruler","Dee","She cried after - not during, after, in the feed room, where she thought nobody would see - that's exactly when you're supposed to cry, nobody teaches that, she figured it out","Marty","Dee cried too","Dee","I cried too, in the other feed room - Missy is delivering a baby with me next month, Lord help us both - if you're expecting, come see us before, not during"}},
{"r9_joke_d310_lige_bread_truck_voice","caller_joke",310,390,16,22,1,{"Caller - Ottis","Marty, I want to say something about the night fellow and I mean it kindly","Marty","Say it kindly then","Caller - Ottis","He sounds exactly like a man reading a bread order - two white, one wheat, four dozen sweet rolls, that's his voice, that's how he says everything","Lige","That's how I said it for six years","Caller - Ottis","And here's the kind part - Tuesday at two in the morning my wife was bad off and I called, and he read me back what I told him in that bread order voice and it was the calmest thing I ever heard in my life","Caller - Ottis","So don't anybody fix him - leave him exactly how he is","Lige","I don't know what to do with that","Marty","Say two white and one wheat, son, say it just like that"}},
{"r9_host_d312_ekron_relay","host",312,400,0,23,1,{"Marty","There's a second station in this county as of Saturday and I want to be the first one to say so, and I want to say it happy","Marty","Two fellows at Ekron have got a rig going out of the old fire hall - they're low and they're scratchy and they don't reach past about six miles, and six miles is exactly the six miles I've never reached","Dee","He's been worried about this for two days and now look at him","Marty","I was worried, I'll admit that on the air, a man gets used to being the only voice and that is not a good thing to get used to","Marty","So here's what we worked out, they take the hollers south of Ekron that I can't get into, and they read my Trading Post and I read theirs, and if either one of us goes off, the other one says why","Marty","That last part is the important part, if this station goes quiet, Ekron will tell you whether it's the diesel or something else - nobody should have to guess about that again"}},
{"r9_mb_d316_bread_route_song","music_bridge",316,400,0,5,1,{"Lige","About here's where Marty says he'd play you something, I got no music either, so I'll do what I do when it's quiet, and you can turn me off if you want","Lige","I say the route, I done it six years and I still say it in my head at two in the morning","Lige","Dock at two, the Sav Rite at three ten - the Ekron market at three forty, the truck stop at four, and the man there always wanted four dozen and took three","Lige","The school at five twenty, and the woman in the cafeteria left the side door propped for me every day for six years and I never once learned her name","Lige","Then back down 31W with the sun coming up behind me and the empty racks rattling - that's it, that's my music","Lige","If anybody out there worked in the cafeteria at the West Point school, I'd like to know her name"}},
{"r9_obit_d317_hollis_bottle_caps","obituary",317,317,8,12,8,{"Reverend Hollis","Good morning, it's the fourth Sunday in May and the window's open behind me, and if you hear birds, that's what that is","Reverend Hollis","Four names, and one of them's a boy, so I'll put him last and you'll know why","Reverend Hollis","Lurline Combs, of West Point, who worked the counter at the drugstore thirty years and knew what every child in that town liked before they got to the stool","Reverend Hollis","Ercel Napier, of the Riverside road, a mail carrier, who kept dog biscuits in the truck at his own expense and had a name for every dog on his route and not one of those names was the dog's real name","Reverend Hollis","Docia Whitt, of Ekron, ninety-one, who buried two husbands and three children and was, up until the twelfth of this month, the one in that family everybody else called when they were low","Reverend Hollis","And Dallas Prather, who was nine years old and collected bottle caps - there's four hundred and some in a coffee can in that house, sorted by color, and I've seen the can","Reverend Hollis","His brother is six and he is still putting caps in the can - nobody in that house has told him to quit and nobody's going to - I'll leave it there"}},
{"r3_sf_d320_kitten_named_lloyd","sign_off",320,400,22,23,1,{"Marty","An update, because the church kids ask on nineteen every single night - yes, the kitten is still here","Marty","I said no, several times, and he sleeps on the transmitter anyway - I guess that's his job now","Dee","I named him Lloyd","Marty","She named him Lloyd, he's already stepped on the knob that says 'DON'T.' Nothing happened - count your people - good night"}},
{"r3_ins_d320_copperheads","instruction",320,400,0,23,1,{"Dee","Snake season, copperheads are thick this year - nobody's mowing, and there are brush piles everywhere, they sit still, and they look just like the leaves","Dee","If you get bit, get away from the snake first so you don't get bit twice - then sit down - keep still - take off rings and your watch before it swells","Dee","Don't cut it, don't suck on it - don't tie it off - don't pack it in ice, every one of those is something a man on nineteen told me his daddy did","Dee","Most copperhead bites won't kill a grown person, they'll hurt like nothing you've ever felt - keep the limb about level with your heart and send for me - children and old folks, send for me faster","Marty","And poke the brush pile with a stick before you reach into it - ain't medicine, just plain sense"}},
{"r5_trade_saturday_market_west_point","trade",320,400,7,13,2,{"Marty","For anybody new to this station, I want to tell you about the Saturday market","Marty","The West Point fire station, every Saturday, eight till noon - bring what you grew, what you made, or what you can fix","Dee","Last week thirty-one tables, one fella sharpening knives, one lady pulling teeth - I won't endorse that one","Marty","There's a fella selling lemonade sweetened with honey, it costs a story, you have to tell him one, and it has to be true","Dee","The volunteers with rifles are up on the roof and at the corners - they're friendly - don't make them less friendly","Marty","When I started this station, I didn't think I'd ever read a market listing again - I'm going to read it every week till somebody makes me stop"}},
{"r3_sil_d322_kitten_on_cord","silence",322,400,10,20,1,{"99.6 FM","(A thump, small claws skittering across a desk, and something batting at the microphone cord)","Marty","That's Lloyd - Lloyd is not on the air - Lloyd is going outside"}},
{"r9_ins_d322_second_summer_heat","instruction",322,400,8,20,1,{"Dee","Heat, you made it through a Kentucky July on the road once, so you think you know this one - two of the three people I lost to heat last summer had already done it once","Dee","Drink before you're thirsty, thirsty is late, a gallon a head on a working day and more if you're out in it, and sip it all day instead of a quart at dinner","Dee","Salt, water on its own all day will make you sick and give you cramps - a pinch in the jug, or eat something salty with it - pickle juice if you've got it and can stand it","Dee","Here's the difference and it matters, soaked with sweat, weak, sick at your stomach, that's heat exhaustion - get in the shade, get wet, get cool, and lay down","Dee","Quit sweating, hot and dry, and talking foolish, that's the other one and that one kills people - get them wet and fanned and cool immediately and come get me at the same time - don't wait to see","Marty","And change the work hours, out at first light, in by eleven, back out at six - I know your fence don't care what time it is - do it anyway","Dee","And check on the old ones twice a day in this - a hot closed-up house with an eighty year old woman in it who says she's fine is the thing I worry about most in the summer"}},
{"r9_host_d326_kid_reads_classifieds","host",326,400,0,23,1,{"Marty","My throat's about gone, I've been talking eleven months for a living after fifty-five years of talking to hogs, so I have hired help","Marty","Tilford's sixteen now, he's read the mail slot for three days and he's better at it than me because he doesn't editorialize","Caller - Tilford","You do it a lot, Marty","Marty","I do - go ahead and read them","Caller - Tilford","Wanted, a hand crank for a Maytag wringer, wanted, anybody who can shoe a horse - for trade, two quilts, wants a crosscut saw, for trade, a laying hen, wants a laying hen, see Vesta","Marty","He read four notes in nineteen seconds, it'd have took me a minute and a half and a story about a wringer","Caller - Tilford","You'd have told the one about your mother's Maytag","Marty","I would have, everybody, the boy's got the slot now, I'll be over here resting my voice and thinking about my mother's Maytag"}},
{"r3_so_d330_lightning_bugs_again","sign_on",330,358,5,7,1,{"Marty","Morning - it's {time}, the lightning bugs came back last night, over the same field out back - first ones I've seen this year","Marty","Last summer I stood on the dock and watched them because I didn't know what else to do - this year I watched them because I knew exactly what I had to do in the morning, and I wanted one minute before it","Marty","I don't know if that's better - it's different, ninety-nine point six - count your people"}},
{"r3_host_d330_somebody_keeping_a_log","host",330,400,9,21,1,{"Marty","Scanner caught something I haven't heard in most of a year - a real dispatcher, or a retired one, working a net upriver","Marty","Unit numbers - ten-codes, somebody asked for a 10-20 and got one - it went on twenty minutes, and I sat here writing down every word like it was the first week","Marty","I don't know who they are, I don't know if they're good people, they sounded organized - there was a time those meant the same thing","Marty","More when I hear it - for now, somebody out there is keeping a log again, and that's enough for me"}},
{"r3_wx_d330_waist_high","weather",330,360,5,10,1,{"Marty","Weather, hot and dry for a week, which in June used to mean one thing to everybody in this county - cut hay","Marty","Nobody's cut hay since the year before last, the fields are waist-high, gone to seed, and full of ticks","Marty","So here's the weather advice - stay out of the tall fields, you can't see what's in them, and some of what's in them is lying down, waiting on something to walk by","Marty","Ninety-one this afternoon, maybe a thunderstorm by evening, lightning bugs tonight, if you want a reason to look out the window"}},
{"r5_trade_roundup_summer_canners","trade",330,400,9,16,1,{"Marty","Summer Trading Post, and it's a hot one, so let's go quick","Marty","Box fans, a man in Muldraugh has three, plus a generator that'll run one of them - he wants a hog - Muldraugh fire station, nine AM","Dee","A woman in West Point has sixty feet of garden hose with no holes - she wants shoes for her boys, sizes four and six - find her at her usual table at the Saturday market","Marty","Somebody in Brandenburg is trading fresh catfish for shotgun shells - you know the rule, and if your name is Gil, we still have questions about that catfish","Dee","Two pressure canners in March Ridge, one with a bad gauge - the good one wants flour, the bad one is free, with a lecture from me","Marty","Out front of Colburn Feed, noon, for the canners - Dee will be there with the lecture","Dee","It's a short lecture, a bad gauge can blow the lid clean off - don't"}},
{"r5_obit_caller_leland_goff_barber","obituary",330,355,8,21,1,{"Caller - Stan","Marty, I'd like to have Leland Goff read, he cut hair in Muldraugh for forty-four years","Caller - Stan","The first day of school every August, he cut every boy's hair for free - line out the door and down past the bank, he told every one of them a joke that wasn't fit for church","Marty","I remember that line, I stood in it in nineteen forty-nine","Caller - Stan","My daddy got his first haircut in that chair - I did, my boy did","Caller - Stan","School would be starting soon, so this week I took my boy down there, sat him in Leland's chair, and cut it myself - bad - real bad","Caller - Stan","I told him the joke, though, the one about the mule - I remembered every word"}},
{"r9_obit_d330_marty_card_on_the_board","obituary",330,400,8,21,1,{"Marty","There's been a card on my corkboard since the fifteenth of August and this morning I took it down, and I want to tell you about it before I put it in the drawer","Marty","It says, LOOKING FOR MY HUSBAND, DRIVES A GREEN LTD, and then a name, and then, I WILL BE AT THIS STORE EVERY FRIDAY","Marty","She came every Friday - forty-some Fridays, she'd read the board and look at me and I'd shake my head and she'd say all right, and then she'd help me sweep before she left","Marty","She died Tuesday, not the way you're thinking - her heart, in her own bed, and Dee was there","Dee","I was there","Marty","So the card comes down, and I want to say her name on this radio, because for forty Fridays she was the one doing the looking and nobody was ever looking for her - her name was Verda Hood","Marty","And if anybody in this county ever sees a green LTD, I'd still like to know about it - that's all, that's the last of that"}},
{"r9_joke_d332_things_i_miss_three","caller_joke",332,400,16,22,1,{"Caller - Mozelle","Marty, three things I miss, and I've had a year to get this list right","Marty","Let's have it","Caller - Mozelle","Ice in a glass, a dial tone - and somebody to be mad at who deserves it","Marty","Say that last one again","Caller - Mozelle","I used to be mad at the water company and the man at the tax office and my sister's husband - every bit of it free and none of it cost me a thing","Caller - Mozelle","Now if I get mad at somebody it's somebody who hauled me a bucket of water yesterday - there's nobody cheap left to be mad at","Marty","You can be mad at me","Caller - Mozelle","Marty, I have been mad at you since the day you said cold water for a burn and then said butter"}},
{"r9_trade_d334_eggs_and_shells","trade",334,400,9,16,1,{"Marty","I want to talk about what things cost now, because folks keep asking me and I've been ducking it","Marty","Nobody's using money, eggs are the small change of this county, a dozen eggs will get you a bar of soap, a jar of anything, or an hour of most people's work","Marty","A gallon of gas is about four dozen eggs, and a gallon of gas is going up every week and will keep going up till there isn't any","Marty","And a .22 shell trades for about what an egg does - I'm going to say that again and then I'm going to say what I think about it - one shell, one egg","Dee","Say it","Marty","I don't like it, I don't like knowing it, I don't like saying it out loud on a radio, and I've sat here nine months refusing to read a shell trade on this station","Marty","But you asked me what things cost and you're entitled to know so nobody cheats you - one shell, one egg, and I'd rather you heard that from me than found it out at a tailgate"}},
{"r3_host_d335_thirty_two","host",335,400,11,13,1,{"Marty","Noon check-in, one breath each","Caller - Joyce","Joyce, Riverside elevator, fourteen, beans are up, and whoever took our tomatoes last summer, we planted extra for you","Marty","Copy, Joyce, fourteen, and extra","Caller - Luis","Luis, West Point, seven now, the baby came Tuesday, a girl, Dee knows","Dee","Dee knows - congratulations, Luis","Caller - Harold","Harold, Ekron road, nine, lost two in March, we're alright, need seed potatoes","Marty","Copy, Harold - nine, I've got seed potatoes, I'm sorry about March","Caller - Terry","Terry, by the quarry - two, her arm's fine, she wanted me to say it's fine","Marty","Copy, Terry, two, and fine, that's thirty-two people I can count - good check-in"}},
{"r5_obit_marty_adele_book_report","obituary",335,400,8,21,3,{"Marty","I've got one to read that I've been dreading","Marty","Miss Adele Pruett, of March Ridge, she taught third grade here for thirty-one years, and since last fall she taught about forty children to read through the side window of the school library","Marty","She was my teacher in nineteen forty-seven, some of you heard her tell me so on this station and heard me go quiet","Dee","He went red, too, I was there","Marty","She said I still owed her a book report, so this spring I read a book on soil and wrote the report out on feed invoices, both sides","Marty","I took it over to her last week, she read it with her glasses down on the end of her nose, gave me a B minus, and said my handwriting hadn't improved since nineteen forty-seven","Marty","She passed on Thursday night, some of her students' mothers were with her, I'm keeping the B minus"}},
{"r9_host_d340_hollis_sunday_ride","host",340,400,0,23,1,{"Marty","Reverend Hollis is in the chair and I want to tell everybody how he got here, because he won't","Reverend Hollis","I'd rather you didn't","Marty","He's seventy-five in August, he can't drive since the pneumonia, so every Sunday morning somebody different drives out to the parsonage and gets him and brings him here, and nobody has ever once been asked to","Reverend Hollis","It's a list - Dee keeps a list","Dee","I keep a list because eleven people wanted the same Sunday and it was turning into a thing","Reverend Hollis","Well, this morning it was a young man in a truck with no muffler on it and a dog in the bed, and he'd swept the seat off for me - I noticed that, I want him to know I noticed that","Marty","Go ahead and read them, Reverend"}},
{"r9_obit_d345_hollis_fathers_day","obituary",345,345,8,12,8,{"Reverend Hollis","Good morning. It's Father's Day. We got through Mother's Day by saying it plain, so we'll do that again","Reverend Hollis","If your daddy's still here, go see him today. If he isn't... mine isn't either. Seventy-four years old, and I still want my daddy","Reverend Hollis","Bud Lawson, of the Echo Creek road, who was on the missing list on this radio for nine months and is not on it anymore, and I'll say no more about how that came out","Reverend Hollis","He taught his girls to drive a straight shift in the church parking lot on Sunday afternoons, all four of them, and the youngest one told me he never once raised his voice about the clutch","Reverend Hollis","Icie Pack, of Muldraugh, who was ninety and who sat on her porch every single evening this spring and waved at everything that went by, including one of them","Reverend Hollis","That's in there because her son told it to me laughing and then he wasn't laughing, and I promised him I'd say it exactly like that","Reverend Hollis","Service is at nine. Fathers, you know which boys in this county haven't got anybody. See about them. That's the sermon, Dee - twenty seconds"}},
{"r9_joke_d346_early_bird_reversal","caller_joke",346,400,16,22,1,{"Caller - Harlan","Marty, my mamaw had a saying for everything and I've been fixing them up to fit","Marty","Go ahead and try one on me","Caller - Harlan","The early bird gets the worm - well, the early bird gets the whole store, because nobody else is up, and the worm's still in the ground where it was","Marty","That's not bad","Caller - Harlan","Here's the one I'm proud of, a stitch in time saves nine - a stitch in time is the only stitch there is, because ain't nobody making thread","Marty","All right, that one's good","Caller - Harlan","I've got about forty of these, my wife has asked me to stop"}},
{"r9_trade_d348_harness_for_a_mule","trade",348,400,9,16,1,{"Caller - Harlan","Marty, I've got a mule and no harness and I'm about to be the only man in this county with a mule and no harness","Marty","What happened to yours?","Caller - Harlan","Mice, two winters in a barn loft and they ate the leather to lace - it's the one thing I didn't think to grease","Marty","What'll you give?","Caller - Harlan","Plowing, whoever finds me a set of work harness gets his garden turned this year and next year, and I'll come to him, and I'll bring the mule and my own dinner","Caller - Harlan","Look in the back of any barn that's got a hay fork hanging in it - somebody's granddaddy's harness is in there under a tarp, and the family that owns it don't know it's there","Dee","And grease the leather you've got, all of you, boots, belts, straps, saddles, once a year and the mice won't bother with it","Marty","A garden turned two years running for a harness out of a loft - that's the best trade on the board and it's going to sit there a month, because nobody looks in their own barn"}},
{"r9_sil_d350_somebody_at_the_dock","silence",350,400,0,23,1,{"Marty","Stand by, folks, there's somebody at the loading dock and I'm needed out there a minute","Marty","All right, that'll be about ten minutes - Lige is going to read the Trading Post while I'm gone","Lige","Wanted, a hand crank for a wringer, wanted, anybody who can shoe a horse, for trade, two quilts","Marty","I'm back, and no, I'm not going to tell you what that was - there's a woman out on my dock and what she's doing out there is hers, not yours and not mine","Marty","If you drive up here and you can't come in, that's fine - there's a chair on the dock, it's there on purpose"}},
{"r9_host_d352_lige_better_at_it","host",352,400,0,23,1,{"Marty","I want to say something about the boy and I'm going to say it while he's stood right there so he can't argue with me later","Marty","Lige is better at the hard calls than I am - I ain't being nice, I've watched it happen about thirty times","Lige","Marty","Marty","When somebody calls in bad off at three in the morning, I want to fix it - I start telling them things - he don't, he asks them what they can see out the window and then he shuts up and lets them talk","Lige","That ain't a skill, I just don't know anything to tell them","Dee","That's the skill, Lige","Marty","That's the skill, twenty-two years old - ask them what they can see and then be quiet - I'm fifty-seven and I'm still learning that off a bread man"}},
{"r9_ins_d356_tired_mistakes","instruction",356,400,8,20,1,{"Marty","This one's for the people who've been at this a year and are good at it - that's who's getting hurt now, and I want to name the way it happens","Marty","One, the same route - you've walked it forty times and it's always been clear, so on the forty-first you walk it reading a note in your hand - that's the one","Lige","I done that on the bread route, same four stops, six years, backed into a pole at the third one because I was thinking about the fourth","Marty","Two, you quit looking up, everybody stares at the road after six months - lift your head on purpose, four times a mile","Marty","Three, you start doing two-man jobs by yourself because waiting on somebody costs a morning - firewood - roofs - wells, that's the one that kills good people in year two","Dee","Four's mine, you quit telling anybody where you went - early on everybody said where they were going, now you've done it a hundred times and you don't bother, and then you don't come back and nobody knows where to start","Marty","Say it out loud at the door, even if the only one listening's a dog - say the road and say when"}},
{"r5_host_fireworks_1994","host",358,361,0,23,5,{"Dee","Somebody down the road set off fireworks last night, and I about came out of my skin","Marty","I thought it was gunfire, I was under the desk before the second one went up","Dee","He was under the desk holding the percolator, the broken percolator, that's what he saved","Marty","I panicked, and I grabbed what I love, there's no shame in it","Dee","Whoever it was: they were pretty, we saw the green ones from the loading dock - please don't do it again","Marty","They heard it too, we counted thirty of them walking up the road by morning"}},
{"r3_sf_d360_one_bottle_rocket","sign_off",360,360,22,23,10,{"Marty","It's the Fourth of July, somebody over toward Rosewood set off one bottle rocket right around dark - just the one","Marty","I've been on this radio since last July telling you not to make noise - and I'll tell you, when I saw that little spark go up, I didn't say a word - I just watched it","Dee","He clapped","Marty","I clapped - very quietly, whoever that was, I hope you were a long way from anything - count your people - good night"}},
{"r9_joke_d360_near_miss_stats","caller_joke",360,400,16,22,1,{"Caller - Lawson","Marty, I've kept a count all year the way you'd keep a count on a ball player, and I'd like to read my numbers","Marty","Read them","Caller - Lawson","Times I've been closer to one of them than I meant to be, thirty-one - times I had to run, nine, times I fell down doing it, six","Marty","Six out of nine","Caller - Lawson","Six out of nine, that's a two-thirds falling average, which would get a man sent down to a lower league","Marty","And what's the number you're proud of?","Caller - Lawson","Times it got hold of me - none, and I'd like to point out that nobody keeps that stat on a card, and it's the only one that counts"}},
{"r5_host_one_year_on_air","host",362,366,0,23,6,{"Marty","It's been a year - July ninth, near as we can tell from the calendar we've been crossing off","Dee","I dug out the first page of my caller chart. How'd I ever get my handwriting that neat?","Marty","Somebody gave me a cassette they taped off the radio that first week - I sounded like a man reading his own will","Dee","You still do, some days, but you laugh now","Marty","I'm not making a speech. If you ever pressed the button, thank you. Even the fella who only called to argue about seed corn","Dee","Especially him, he was right","Marty","He was not right, ninety-nine point six, March Ridge - year two starts now, let's see how we do"}},
{"r9_trade_d364_letters_in_stone","trade",364,400,9,16,1,{"Caller - Garnett","Marty, I've been cutting letters in stone since March and I want it read out on the radio, and I'd like you to let me say it my way","Marty","Go ahead","Caller - Garnett","I did Maudie's first, took me eleven days and it's crooked and the D is bigger than the rest - then a man asked me to do his wife and I said no, and then I thought about it a week and went and did it","Caller - Garnett","I use creek rock and a masonry chisel and I can do a name, two dates and one line - not a verse - one line, anything longer than that and the rock splits, or I do","Marty","And what are you asking?","Caller - Garnett","Food, whatever's in your house, and I'd rather it was something cooked - I'm not going to take a thing off a family that hasn't got it, and I'll be the judge of who hasn't got it","Dee","Garnett, how many have you done?","Caller - Garnett","Nineteen - get word to me through Marty, and write out exactly how you want the name spelled, in your own hand, because I'll copy whatever you send me and I won't guess"}},
{"r3_so_d365_one_year","sign_on",365,365,5,9,12,{"Marty","Good morning, it's Saturday, July the ninth, nineteen ninety-four - one year","Marty","I spent about a month working out what to say this morning - I had it written down on feed tickets - Dee took them away from me","Dee","He was going to read them in order","Marty","So here's what's left without the tickets, we're still here, you're still there - it's hot, the diesel's low, the cat on the transmitter isn't the same cat","Marty","I still don't know a lot, but I know the name of near about every one of you who's keyed up on nineteen this year, and a year ago I didn't know a single one - count your people - it's {time}"}},
{"r3_sf_d365_not_alone","sign_off",365,365,22,23,12,{"Marty","That's one year, I'm going to shut this off in a minute, same as the first night, to save fuel","Marty","The first night I told you I was alone and the cat didn't count - I'm not alone, there's a nurse asleep in the second chair with her boots on, a kitten named after a dead man on the transmitter, and about two hundred of you","Marty","Nancy, if by some chance, the store's still open - sort of, I kept the books - badly","Marty","Count your people, check your locks, and be here tomorrow - this is Marty Colburn, ninety-nine point six, March Ridge - good night"}},
{"r3_host_d366_hollis_second_list","host",366,366,9,12,10,{"Reverend Hollis","Good morning, a year and a day, I've come down to this feed store most Sundays for fifty weeks of it - I missed some for the pneumonia - Marty covered, he did poorly","Marty","I did fine","Reverend Hollis","He did fine, I've been asked to say something about the year, and I'll say the one thing I'm sure of - I buried more people this year than in all forty years before it","Reverend Hollis","I also married two couples, and baptized three babies in Otter Creek, none of them nearly drowned","Reverend Hollis","I'm not going to tell you it evens out - it doesn't, I'm only telling you the second list exists - go add something to the second list"}},
{"r3_wx_d366_feed_sack_forecast","weather",366,400,5,10,1,{"Dee","Weather from the night shift, because Marty's lost his voice again - he's writing it on a feed sack and holding it up for me","Dee","Sack says 'Hot, ninety-four, no rain' - and under it, 'Tell them to drink water!!!' So... drink water","Dee","Now he's writing something else, it says, 'Same as last year.' And he's crossed out 'last year' and written 'always'","Dee","I'm told that's the weather, I'm told that's the forecast from here on out - good morning"}},
{"r3_sil_d366_left_on_for_company","silence",366,400,0,4,1,{"99.6 FM","(Only the carrier, steady, for a long time, somebody left it on, so there would be something on ninety-nine point six)"}},
{"r5_obit_hollis_one_year_three_names","obituary",366,366,8,12,8,{"Reverend Hollis","Good morning, a year ago today I stood in my kitchen in front of a television showing nothing but static, and I didn't know what a Sunday was going to mean anymore","Reverend Hollis","Here's what it has meant, it's meant this microphone, and Marty's coffee, and your names","Reverend Hollis","I went back through the book this week, there are one thousand and eleven names in it, I've read every one of them on this station","Reverend Hollis","I won't read them again, instead I'm going to read three names that came in this week that aren't in that book at all","Reverend Hollis","Joseph Wade Carter, born the second of July, in Riverside - Ella Faith Bybee, born the twenty-fifth of June, at a camp near Ekron","Reverend Hollis","And a boy in West Point whose parents haven't settled on a name yet, and would welcome suggestions","Reverend Hollis","A thousand and eleven, and three, this morning I'll take the three - go in peace, Knox County"}},
{"r9_host_d367_day_after","host",367,372,0,23,1,{"Marty","Yesterday was a year and a day, and I've got nothing prepared, and that is on purpose","Marty","Everybody said the big thing yesterday, today's a Sunday in July and the beans need water and there's a woman on the Ekron road with a tooth that's got to come out","Dee","It's coming out this afternoon","Marty","I spent about a month working up to yesterday and then it was over by nine in the morning - and the truth is I felt better today than I did yesterday and I've been trying to work out why","Marty","Here's what I've got, yesterday I had to think about a year - today I've only got to think about Thursday - Thursday's a size a man can pick up","Marty","So Thursday: water the beans, pull the tooth, read the slot, and count your people"}},
{"r9_so_d368_second_summer","sign_on",368,400,5,8,1,{"Marty","Good morning, it's {time} and it's already close on eighty - second summer","Marty","There's people hearing this that weren't hearing it last July - some of you walked in here from other counties and some of you just now got a radio that works, and I want to say the four things I say for new folks","Marty","One, I'm a man in a feed store and I don't know anything you don't - two, the nurse is real and she's free, three, we trade in daylight out in the open - four, nobody is coming, I say that plain so you don't spend a year waiting","Dee","Say the fifth one","Marty","Five, this county will feed you if you'll work, and I can put you with somebody today - that's the five - count your people"}},
{"r3_host_d370_until_noon_is_just_me","host",370,400,19,22,1,{"Marty","A few of you have asked if the station's still worth the diesel - I've asked myself that, lying on the cot","Marty","That diesel could run Harold's well pump, it could run the saw at the church - it could get a family to wherever they think they're going","Marty","So here's what I've decided, I'll keep it on as long as somebody keys up at noon with a number - the day the noon check-in is just me, I'll shut it off and give Harold the cans","Dee","It won't be just you","Marty","Then I'll see you at noon"}},
{"r9_mb_d370_kitchen_tape","music_bridge",370,400,0,23,1,{"Marty","A woman brought me a cassette tape and asked me to play about a minute of it, and I listened to it first, and then I said yes","Marty","There's no music on it, her daughter got a tape recorder for Christmas in eighty-six and went around the house taping things, the way a kid will","Marty","This is a minute of her mother's kitchen, that's a screen door - that's a whisk on a bowl, there's a dog in there somewhere and a car going by","Marty","Nobody talks on it, she said that's why she can stand to play it","Marty","That's the minute, if you've got a tape of nothing in your house, don't you throw it out - that's what the nothing sounds like"}},
{"r9_sf_d372_one_year_of_nights","sign_off",372,400,22,23,1,{"Marty","Last thing before Lige takes it - Dee added up the composition books tonight","Dee","Four thousand and some calls in the books, he's done a little over three thousand of them","Lige","I'd have said it was more","Marty","A year ago there was one radio in this county and a man who didn't know how to work it - now there's a boy with a numbered book and a nurse with a bicycle and me, and I'm the least useful of the three","Dee","You're the voice, Marty, that counts for something","Marty","It counts for something - good night - count your people"}},
{"r9_host_d378_things_i_dont_say","host",378,400,0,23,1,{"Marty","I've got a page in the back of the ledger of things I used to say on this radio and don't say anymore - I read it over this morning and I want to read you three of them","Marty","One, when this is over, I said that about forty times in July and August - I quit in October and I couldn't tell you the day I quit","Marty","Two, any word from Louisville, I said that every morning for two months - there's no word from Louisville, there hasn't been and I've made my peace with not asking","Marty","Three, I'm sure somebody's working on it - I'd like to apologize for that one, that was me handing you something I didn't have","Dee","Read the other side of the page","Marty","There's things on the other side I say now that I never did - the nurse is free - nobody's coming, and, I don't know, which took me the longest to learn of anything on either page"}},
{"r9_obit_d380_hollis_made_the_winter","obituary",380,400,8,12,8,{"Reverend Hollis","Good morning, it's the end of July and it is ninety-some degrees in this building, and I've buried four people this month who got all the way through that winter","Reverend Hollis","I want to say that plain because it's the hardest thing I know right now - they got through January in a house with no furnace, and then it got hot and they didn't","Reverend Hollis","Eula Kite, of Rosewood, seventy-eight, who hauled her own water up a hill all winter and would not let a neighbor do it because she said she needed the walk","Reverend Hollis","Orval Sizemore, of Ekron, who had the garage and who has been on this radio a dozen times telling men how to keep their trucks running - he worked on somebody else's truck the morning he went","Reverend Hollis","Cordie Halbert, of March Ridge, who whistled on this radio one night because her daddy whistled - some of you will remember it, she had Tuesdays for a while there","Reverend Hollis","Do what Dee says about the heat, I'm not going to preach it, she's already said it better than I could - check on the old ones twice a day and don't you take fine for an answer","Reverend Hollis","Four names, all of them got through the winter, and I got no lesson in it - I just won't let it pass unsaid"}},
{"r9_joke_d384_anniversary_cake","caller_joke",384,400,16,22,1,{"Caller - Roxie","Marty, I made a cake for the ninth of July and I have been catching it from my whole family for two weeks","Marty","You made a cake","Caller - Roxie","A stack cake, with the last of the dried apples - my son-in-law said, what in the world are you celebrating","Marty","And what'd you say?","Caller - Roxie","I told him nine of us sat down at that table, and last July the ninth there was eleven of us and we didn't know what was coming, and this year there's nine and we do","Caller - Roxie","And then I said, so eat your cake","Marty","Did he eat it?","Caller - Roxie","He had two pieces and he cried at the table, and I let him, and then I gave him the pan to wash"}},
{"r9_trade_d386_well_driller","trade",386,400,9,16,1,{"Marty","This is the biggest trade I've ever read on this station and I want everybody to stop what they're doing and listen at it","Caller - Talmadge","I drilled wells in this county thirty-four years, I've got the rig, the bits, the pipe, and a boy who's worked with me since he was fourteen","Caller - Talmadge","What I don't have is diesel, that rig eats about forty gallon to put a well down, and that's if we hit water where I think it is","Marty","So the trade is forty gallons of diesel for a well","Caller - Talmadge","Forty gallon and a crew of four to handle pipe - and I'll go where the most people are, not where the most diesel is - I want that understood before anybody starts pooling fuel","Caller - Talmadge","A hand-dug well will run dry in August, a drilled well won't - I'd rather put down two good wells this year than six holes for folks that can pay","Dee","That means a town or a road pools it together - one family can't do forty gallon and shouldn't try","Marty","So talk to your neighbors and come to me with a road and a number of houses - this one's not first call gets it, this one we're all going to sit down and argue about, out loud, on the air"}},
}
